      "type": "text",
      "text": "{\"pair_id\": \"Menhet|701989\", \"verdict\": \"different_name\", \"reasoning\": \"Record 2 reads Mn-Jḥy as m, n, then j, ḥ, y. In Menhet, M matches m and n matches n. The j can be left out, h matches ḥ, and the y can be left out. The vowel e's stand for no sign. But Menhet ends in a t, and the transliteration has no t at all. The rule that lets a t be omitted covers only a t that is in the transliteration and missing from the modern spelling, not the reverse. This t is a consonant left over in record 1, so the names differ.\"}"
     }
